  "text_md": "20 K. FANG AND Z. JIA\n\ndue to the loss of orthogonality of basis vectors. Thus we set the maximum iteration steps to 10n. We have observed that for the test problems in our experiments, 10n is enough to ensure convergence. We perform full reorthogonalization on Vk′, Uk and Uk to ensure that they are numerically orthonormal to the working precision ǫ. By default, the convergence of line 2 in Algorithm 6.1 is judged by (3.18) with the stopping tolerance tol = 10−8. The starting vector u1 is randomly generated by the standard normal distribution function randn in MATLAB and then normalized.\n\n![](<2503.04041_pg20_images/imageFile1.png>)\n\nParameters Default values Description target 5 |target| = l is the number of desired\n\n![](<2503.04041_pg20_images/imageFile2.png>)\n\nGSVD components, where target > 0 or target < 0 means that the largest or smallest GSVD components are required.\n\n![](<2503.04041_pg20_images/imageFile3.png>)\n\nkmax Maximum subspace dimension adjust 3 Integer added to l to speed up\n\n![](<2503.04041_pg20_images/imageFile4.png>)\n\nconvergence tol 10−8 Stopping tolerance maxit 1000 Maximum number of restarts lsqrtol 10ǫ Stopping tolerance of lsqr lsqrmaxit 10n Maximum number of iterations of lsqr u1 generated by randn\n\n![](<2503.04041_pg20_images/imageFile5.png>)\n\n![](<2503.04041_pg20_images/imageFile6.png>)\n\n![](<2503.04041_pg20_images/imageFile7.png>)\n\n![](<2503.04041_pg20_images/imageFile8.png>)\n\n![](<2503.04041_pg20_images/imageFile9.png>)\n\nThe unit length starting vector\n\nand then normalized\n\n![](<2503.04041_pg20_images/imageFile10.png>)\n\nTable 1 Parameters of IRJBD\n\n7. Numerical experiments. We now present a number of numerical experiments to demonstrate the performance of Algorithm 6.1, written in MATLAB language, and compare it with the public C language code of TRJBD in [1]. The experiments will illustrate that Algorithm 6.1 is at least competitive with and can outperform TRJBD considerably in terms of restarts. Since section 4.4 has shown that each restart of IRJBD and TRJBD basically costs the same for the same l and kmax, fewer restarts mean higher eﬃciency. The experiments were performed on an AMD Ryzen 7 5800X CPU with 78 GB RAM and 8 cores using the MATLAB R2024a with the machine precision ǫ = 2.22 × 10−16 under the Ubuntu 20.04.3 LTS 64-bit system.\n\nWe use some matrices from the SuiteSparse Matrix Collection [6] as A, and L is taken as (cf. [11]):\n\n\n\n\n\n3 1 1\n\n...\n\n...\n\n,\n\nL =\n\n \n\n \n\n...\n\n... 1 1 3\n\nwhich is well conditioned. Table 2 lists the test matrices A and some of their properties, where nnz is the total number of nonzero entries of {A,L} and κ(A) = σ\n\nmax(A) σmin(A) , available from the SuiteSparse Matrix Collection, is the condition number of A and indicates the level of ill-conditioning of A and reﬂects magnitudes of generalized singular values of {A,L}. In order to ensure that all the A are ﬂat or square, i.e., m n, we transpose some matrices, denoted by superscript T. Note that the smallest singular value of kneser 8 3 1T is tiny, which causes that the matrix is numerically row\n\n![](<2503.04041_pg20_images/imageFile11.png>)\n\n![](<2503.04041_pg20_images/imageFile12.png>)\n\n![](<2503.04041_pg20_images/imageFile13.png>)\n\n![](<2503.04041_pg20_images/imageFile14.png>)\n\n",
